@page "/lines-and-areas"

<PageTitle>Lines and areas · RioCharts</PageTitle>

<h1>Lines and areas</h1>
<p class="lead">Time series are the common case: lines with three curve shapes, gaps for missing values, and areas that start at zero. The x axis can also be plain numbers.</p>

<Example Title="Curves" Code="@CurvesCode">
    <Description>
        <p><code>Linear</code> (default), <code>Monotone</code> (smooth, never overshoots the data) and <code>Step</code> (each value holds until the next point, for limits, prices and states). Here web sessions, and the server capacity that is scaled up for the day and down at night.</p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="row">
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="200" Title="Monotone">
                <LineSeries Items="WebSessions" Name="Web sessions" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            </RioChart>
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="200" Title="Step">
                <LineSeries Items="Capacity" Name="Capacity" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Curve="CurveType.Step" />
            </RioChart>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Gaps" Code="@GapsCode">
    <Description>
        <p>A <code>null</code> y value breaks the line instead of dropping to zero: here the conversion rate is missing for a few hours while tracking was down. A point with no neighbour gets a small dot so it stays visible.</p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart Items="Conversion" Height="220" YAxis="Percent">
            <LineSeries Name="Conversion" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0.00 '%'" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Irregular time series" Code="@IrregularCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Points are placed at their real times, so series do not need a fixed interval or the same timestamps. Here the stock of a product
            is logged only when it has moved by 3 items, or after an hour without a reading (@StockOnChange.Count readings: many while it
            sells fast and when a delivery comes in, few at night), and the reorder level is a step line that changes at irregular times.
            Hover a stock reading: the tooltip also lists the reorder level in effect at that moment, marked on the step line at the point
            where that level was set.
        </p>
        <p>
            A long silence is not a gap by itself: the warehouse system was offline 13:10–15:05 and the line simply joins the readings on
            either side. To break the line, add a reading with a <code>null</code> value where the data stops (see Gaps above).
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="260" YAxis="StockAxis">
            <LineSeries Items="StockOnChange" Name="Stock" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0 'in stock'" />
            <LineSeries Items="ReorderLevel" Name="Reorder level" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0" Curve="CurveType.Step" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Numbers on the x axis" Code="@NumericCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            The x values do not have to be times: when <code>XValue</code> returns a number, the x axis is a linear number axis with nice
            ticks, and everything else works the same (curves, gaps, zoom, the tooltip, reference lines). Here a load test: the response
            time at each step from 10 to 500 concurrent users. The 95th percentile stays under the 120 ms limit up to about 350 users, then
            climbs steeply as the servers saturate. <code>XAxis</code> takes a title and a <code>Format</code> for the tick labels, and the
            vertical <code>ReferenceLine</code> with <code>Axis="AnnotationAxis.X"</code> marks the expected peak load.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart Items="LoadTest" Height="260" XAxis="UsersAxis" YAxis="Milliseconds">
            <LineSeries Name="Median" XValue="s => s.Users" YValue="s => s.MedianMs" Format="0 ms" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <LineSeries Name="95th percentile" XValue="s => s.Users" YValue="s => s.P95Ms" Format="0 ms" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <ReferenceLine Value="120" Label="Limit" />
            <ReferenceLine Axis="AnnotationAxis.X" Value="300" Label="Expected peak" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Areas" Code="@AreaCode">
    <Description><p>Areas always include zero, with a light fill under the line.</p></Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart Items="Revenue" Height="220" YAxis="Euros">
            <AreaSeries Name="Revenue" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="#,0 €" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Colors by value" Code="@BandsCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            <code>ColorBands</code> colors a line by its value: here response times turn red above 120 ms, exactly where the line crosses
            the limit, also between two readings. Bands take <code>Above</code>, <code>Below</code> or both; later bands win where they
            overlap. On areas the fill follows, on bars and scatter points each one takes the color of its band, and the tooltip dot
            matches. In JSON specs: <code>"colorBands": [{ "above": 120, "color": "--rio-negative" }]</code>.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart Items="ResponseMs" Height="220" YAxis="Milliseconds">
            <LineSeries Name="Checkout" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0 ms"
                        ColorBands="@([new ColorBand { Above = 120, Color = "--rio-negative" }])" />
            <ReferenceLine Value="120" Label="Limit" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Stacked areas" Code="@StackedCode">
    <Description><p>Series with the same <code>Stack</code> name are stacked: sessions by channel add up to the total. Hide one in the legend and the stack closes up.</p></Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="240" YAxis="Sessions">
            <AreaSeries Items="Search" Name="Search" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="channels" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <AreaSeries Items="Social" Name="Social" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="channels" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <AreaSeries Items="Email" Name="Email" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="channels" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Range areas" Code="@RangeCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            <code>RangeAreaSeries</code> fills a band between a low and a high value per x: here the lowest and highest revenue on each date over
            the last three years, around the average line. The tooltip shows "low – high". In a JSON spec it is <code>"type": "rangeArea"</code>
            with <code>"low"</code> and <code>"y"</code> fields; with <code>timeBucket</code> the band keeps the lowest low and highest high.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart Items="RevenueHistory" Height="260" YAxis="RevenueAxis">
            <RangeAreaSeries Name="Last 3 years" XValue="d => d.Date" LowValue="d => d.Low" HighValue="d => d.High" Format="#,0 €" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <LineSeries Name="Average" XValue="d => d.Date" YValue="d => d.Average" Format="#,0 €" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="Edge cases" Code="@EdgeCode">
    <Description><p>Empty data, a single point and all-equal values each render a clean state, never an error or a collapsed axis.</p></Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="row">
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="150" Title="Empty">
                <LineSeries Items="_empty" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
            </RioChart>
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="150" Title="One point">
                <LineSeries Items="_one" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
            </RioChart>
            <RioChart TItem="Reading" Height="150" Title="All equal">
                <LineSeries Items="_flat" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" />
            </RioChart>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

@code {
    private static readonly AxisSpec Percent = new() { Title = "%" };
    private static readonly AxisSpec Milliseconds = new() { Title = "Response time, ms" };

    private const string BandsCode = """
        <RioChart Items="responseTimes">
            <LineSeries Name="Checkout" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0 ms"
                        ColorBands="@([new ColorBand { Above = 120, Color = "--rio-negative" }])" />
            <ReferenceLine Value="120" Label="Limit" />
        </RioChart>
        """;
    private static readonly AxisSpec StockAxis = new() { Title = "Items" };
    private static readonly AxisSpec Euros = new() { Title = "€ per 15 min" };
    private static readonly AxisSpec Sessions = new() { Title = "Sessions per 15 min" };
    private static readonly AxisSpec RevenueAxis = new() { Title = "Revenue per day" };
    private static readonly List<Reading> _empty = [];
    private static readonly List<Reading> _one = [new(Start, 120)];
    private static readonly List<Reading> _flat = Enumerable.Range(0, 24).Select(h => new Reading(Start.AddHours(h), 200)).ToList();

    private const string CurvesCode = """
        <LineSeries Items="web" Name="Web sessions" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
        <LineSeries Items="capacity" Name="Capacity" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Curve="CurveType.Step" />
        """;

    private const string GapsCode = """
        <RioChart Items="conversion" YAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "%" })">
            <LineSeries Name="Conversion" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0.00 '%'" />  @* Value is double? *@
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string IrregularCode = """
        @* Readings logged on change: no fixed interval, different timestamps per series. *@
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" YAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "Items" })">
            <LineSeries Items="stock" Name="Stock" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0 'in stock'" />
            <LineSeries Items="reorderLevel" Name="Reorder level" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="0" Curve="CurveType.Step" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private static readonly AxisSpec UsersAxis = new() { Title = "Concurrent users", Format = "0" };

    private const string NumericCode = """
        @* public record LoadStep(double Users, double MedianMs, double P95Ms); *@
        <RioChart Items="loadTest" XAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "Concurrent users", Format = "0" })"
                  YAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "Response time, ms" })">
            <LineSeries Name="Median" XValue="s => s.Users" YValue="s => s.MedianMs" Format="0 ms" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <LineSeries Name="95th percentile" XValue="s => s.Users" YValue="s => s.P95Ms" Format="0 ms" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <ReferenceLine Value="120" Label="Limit" />
            <ReferenceLine Axis="AnnotationAxis.X" Value="300" Label="Expected peak" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string AreaCode = """
        <RioChart Items="revenue" YAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "€ per 15 min" })">
            <AreaSeries Name="Revenue" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Format="#,0 €" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string StackedCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" YAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "Sessions per 15 min" })">
            <AreaSeries Items="search" Name="Search" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="channels" />
            <AreaSeries Items="social" Name="Social" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="channels" />
            <AreaSeries Items="email" Name="Email" XValue="r => r.Time" YValue="r => r.Value" Stack="channels" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string RangeCode = """
        <RioChart Items="revenueHistory" YAxis="@(new AxisSpec { Title = "Revenue per day" })">
            <RangeAreaSeries Name="Last 3 years" XValue="d => d.Date" LowValue="d => d.Low" HighValue="d => d.High"
                             Format="#,0 €" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
            <LineSeries Name="Average" XValue="d => d.Date" YValue="d => d.Average" Format="#,0 €" Curve="CurveType.Monotone" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string EdgeCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Title="Empty"><LineSeries Items="empty" ... /></RioChart>
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Title="One point"><LineSeries Items="one" ... /></RioChart>
        <RioChart TItem="Reading" Title="All equal"><LineSeries Items="flat" ... /></RioChart>
        """;
}
